   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-2-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Which of these code snippets is correct based on this lesson, if you want to check for the presence of missing values in your dataset? Suppose the dataset is stored in a variable named 'dataset' which is a Pandas DataFrame object.</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>dataset.isnull().sum()</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. This lesson checks for missing data by calling isnull().sum() on the dataframe.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>findMissing(dataset)</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. This lesson does not use findMissing. It checks for missing data with isnull().sum().</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>sum(null(dataset))</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. This lesson calls isnull() on the dataframe and then sum(), as in pumpkins.isnull().sum().</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-2-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Which of these plotting methods is useful when you would like to understand the spread of different groups of datapoints from your dataset?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Scatter Plot</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The lesson says this scatter plot is not very useful. To see each group, like each month, you group the data into a bar chart.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Line Plot</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A line plot was not the clearest choice for this noisy data. Bar charts are used to show distributions.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Bar Plot</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A bar chart groups the data, such as price by month. Seaborn's bar chart also shows how much the price varies within each month.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
